module std.core.shareable;

export {
	Share
};

/// Trait for shared-owner types that can produce another OWNER of
/// the same underlying resource.
///
/// `share` is intentionally distinct from `copy`:
///
/// - `copy` (only for `Copy` types) produces a value-like duplicate
///   with no aliasing — the result behaves as an independent value.
/// - `share` (only for `Share` types) produces another OWNER of the
///   same underlying thing.  Aliasing is part of the semantic.
///   Mutations through either owner are observable through the other.
///
/// `share` is a **warning-bearing** operation: choosing to call it
/// is choosing to accept aliasing.  If the underlying resource is
/// observed or mutated across threads, tasks, or callbacks,
/// synchronization/serialization is the programmer's responsibility.
/// `Share` says nothing about thread-safety of mutations performed
/// through aliased owners.
///
/// `Share` is NOT a synonym for `Clone`.  Drift deliberately keeps
/// "value-like duplication" (`Copy`) and "shared-owner duplication"
/// (`Share`) as distinct capabilities — they answer different
/// questions and carry different programmer guarantees.  A type
/// implements one, the other, both (rare and meaningful), or
/// neither.
///
/// **First adopter:** `Arc<T>` (in `std.concurrent`).
///
/// **String stays on `copy`, not `share`.**  Even though `String`
/// is implemented with shared backing under the hood, its
/// user-facing semantic is value-like duplication — `copy s`
/// produces a value the user can treat as their own.  Forcing
/// `String` into `Share` would conflate the two capabilities and
/// defeat the warning value of the `share` keyword.
///
/// **Closure share-capture syntax:**
///
///     |req, ctx| captures(share app) => { app.handle(req, ctx) }
///
/// At closure-construction time, the compiler evaluates
/// `app.share()` once, move-captures the resulting owned value into
/// the closure environment, and leaves the outer `app` binding
/// usable.
pub trait Share {
	/// Produce another owner of the same underlying resource.
	///
	/// Aliasing is intentional.  If the resource is mutated through
	/// any owner across threads/tasks/callbacks, synchronization is
	/// the caller's responsibility.
	fn share(self: &Self) nothrow -> Self;
}
